<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>BGTaskSchedulerPermittedIdentifiers</key>
	<array>
		<string>com.kmptemplate.maintenance</string>
	</array>
	<key>CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	  The app's name is NOT here, and putting it back here does not work.

	  GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE is YES, and a key Xcode synthesizes silently
	  overrides the same key in this file. A CFBundleName set here loses to the
	  generated $(PRODUCT_NAME). This file used to carry one with a comment
	  saying it was load-bearing; it never reached a single build, and a
	  downstream app shipped under its old name because of it.
	  INFOPLIST_KEY_CFBundleName does not rescue it either: Xcode accepts the
	  setting and then ignores it, verified against the built plist.

	  So PRODUCT_NAME in Configuration/Config.xcconfig is the only lever, and it
	  is the name App Store delivery checks for uniqueness (ITMS-90129). Rename
	  the app there. CFBundleDisplayName, the home-screen name, is separate and
	  is INFOPLIST_KEY_CFBundleDisplayName in the target's build settings.
	-->
	<key>NSUserNotificationsUsageDescription</key>
	<string>We'd like to send you notifications.</string>
	<!--
	  The export compliance answer, stated once here instead of by hand on every
	  upload. Without it a build finishes processing and then sits in TestFlight
	  as "Missing Compliance", installable by nobody, until someone opens App
	  Store Connect and answers the encryption question. Nothing warns you: the
	  upload succeeds, the build goes VALID, and the testers just never get it.
	  Two downstream apps were sitting in that state at once on 2026-09-25.

	  False is correct for an app whose only encryption is HTTPS through the
	  system's own TLS, which Apple exempts (Category 5 Part 2). That covers
	  crash reporting, telemetry and ad networks. An app that implements or
	  bundles a cipher, or that ships its own TLS, has to change this key and
	  file the paperwork behind it. Check before assuming you inherited the
	  right answer.
	-->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
	<!--
	  REQUIRED, even though nothing in the generated app calls the camera.

	  :libraries:ui ships a CameraPreview across all three source sets and
	  IOSNativeViewFactory.swift imports AVFoundation to serve it. App Store
	  delivery scans the binary for *API references*, not for call sites, so the
	  upload is rejected with ITMS-90683 whether or not a screen ever shows a
	  preview. A downstream app hit exactly this on its first submission.

	  **This key is a stopgap and should not survive.** It is here so a fresh
	  project uploads at all; it is the wrong answer for almost every app,
	  because it declares access to a sensor the app never touches and so
	  contradicts the App Privacy answers and PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy. The camera
	  is coming out of the template wholesale, Android permission included:
	  docs/PORT-CANDIDATES.md, "Remove the camera entirely", lists every file.

	  Until that lands:

	  * **Using the camera.** Keep this key and rewrite the string to say what
	    you use it for. A vague purpose string is its own rejection.
	  * **Not using the camera**, which is most apps. Do the deletion in that
	    entry now rather than shipping this key. Confirm with `otool -L` on the
	    built .app that AVFoundation is gone.
	-->
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>processing</string>
	</array>
	<!--
	  Custom-scheme deep links. `kmptemplate://login-callback` is the browser
	  OAuth return trip and `kmptemplate://auth/confirmed` the verify-email
	  redirect — .onOpenURL in iOSApp.swift forwards both to DeepLinkBridge.

	  Universal Links (https://...) require an `Associated Domains`
	  entitlement (`applinks:example.com`) plus an
	  apple-app-site-association file at https://example.com/.well-known/.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>com.kmptemplate</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>kmptemplate</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
